.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-TOUCH-OBJECT 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-touch-object \- re-send notifications for objects already in a bucket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm touch-object
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-prefix | \-p
.IR KEY-PREFIX ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Announces objects that are already in a bucket, as though each had just
been uploaded \- the same
.I esm.object.created
event and the same bucket subscription deliveries an upload would have
produced.
.SH IT DOES NOT TOUCH THE OBJECT
Nothing about the objects changes. Not a byte of them, and not their
timestamps either.
.PP
"Touch" here means what it does to listeners, not what it does to
storage. A modified time is something consumers compare against to
decide what is new, so moving it would make this destructive in exactly
the way it is meant not to be \- the whole point is to repair what
listeners heard without disturbing what is stored.
.SH WHEN THIS IS THE RIGHT REPAIR
A notification can be lost in ways an object never is:
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
a consumer was down while its subscription was
.I live
rather than durable, so its events were pushed to nobody and are gone;
.IP \(bu 3
the queue a bucket subscription delivered into was deleted while
deliveries were still queued for it;
.IP \(bu 3
a subscription was created after the objects had already arrived, so it
never heard about them at all.
.RE
.PP
In each of those the data is intact and only the announcement was lost.
Re-uploading gigabytes to re-send a few kilobytes of notification is the
wrong repair; this is the right one.
.SH WHAT IS SELECTED
The prefix is matched against the whole key, so it selects
.PP
.RS
.nf
--prefix jvo/incoming/       a directory
--prefix jvo/incoming/a.xml  one object
--prefix jvo                 everything under jvo
.fi
.RE
.PP
and omitting it announces every object in the bucket, which on a large
one is a great many notifications. The answer says how many were sent,
so the size of what just happened is visible:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "ern": "...:bucket:onix3-tmp-parsing-input",
  "bucketName": "onix3-tmp-parsing-input",
  "prefix": "jvo/incoming/",
  "objects": 214
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The prefix is a literal, not a pattern \- a "." in it matches a "." and
nothing else.
.PP
Directory markers are left out, since an upload never announces those
either.
.SH THE CONSUMERS MUST BE IDEMPOTENT
This is a replay, and euclid does not know which objects a given
subscriber already handled \- it has no record of what any consumer did
with an event, only that it was delivered. So every subscriber of the
bucket hears about every selected object, including the ones it
processed the first time.
.PP
A consumer that is idempotent \- one for which handling the same object
twice leaves the same result as handling it once \- is unaffected. One
that is not will do its work twice: a second row inserted, a second
file written, a second message sent onward. On a whole bucket it will
do that for everything in it.
.PP
Two things follow:
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
narrow the prefix to what was actually missed. Touching a directory is
almost always safer than touching a bucket, and touching one key is
safer still;
.IP \(bu 3
if a consumer is not idempotent and cannot be made so, take its
subscription out of the way first \- unsubscribe it, or stop the
application \- and put it back afterwards.
.RE
.PP
Nothing here is undoable once the notifications have gone out, so this
is worth a moment before rather than after.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME\fR
Bucket holding the objects. Required. A bucket name is enough; a full
ERN also works and is what reaches another namespace.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix " " \fIKEY-PREFIX\fR
Only objects whose key starts with this. Omit for every object in the
bucket.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The notifications were sent. The count is printed as JSON, including
when nothing matched.
.TP
.B 1
Missing arguments, no session, the bucket does not exist, it belongs to
another account, the caller has no grant for it, or the server rejected
the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Re-announce one directory after fixing a consumer that missed it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm touch-object \e
    --bucket onix3-tmp-parsing-input \e
    --prefix jvo/incoming/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Re-announce a single object:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm touch-object \e
    --bucket onix3-tmp-parsing-input \e
    --prefix jvo/incoming/pim-3056.xml
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-objects (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-redrive-dlq (1)
